+        # imports numpy/numba/scipy, which registers millions of tracked
+        # objects.  A gen2 collection then stalls the real-time loops for
+        # ~50 ms about once per second.  freeze() moves everything that
+        # exists right now (all module objects) out of GC's tracking, and
+        # the raised thresholds make the remaining sweeps rare and cheap.
+        gc.collect()
+        gc.freeze()
+        gc.set_threshold(50000, 1000, 1000)
+        # Freeze+thresholds alone are not enough: the loop's fresh objects
+        # still get promoted and re-scanned every ~1 s, costing 35-50 ms per
+        # sweep (measured).  The control loops create only refcounted numpy
+        # arrays/dicts (no reference cycles), so the cyclic collector can be
